    "product_id": "dd6f9de0-37bd-45a3-8c62-d7a5154427fa",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 031745\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1045 AM MST Sat Oct 3 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 18Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- An unseasonably strong ridge of high pressure will boost\n  temperatures around 3-5 degrees above normal this weekend and\n  eventually into a well above normal range by the middle of next\n  week as lower desert highs reach up to 98-105 degrees.\n\n- Periods of breezy to locally windy conditions can be expected\n  across southcentral and southwestern AZ through Monday with \n  gusts reaching 20-30 mph at times.\n\n- Dry weather with zero rain chances will persist through this \n  weekend and into much of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nLatest GOES mid-lvl wv imagery and 500 mb objective analysis reveals \na strong area of high pressure building over N AZ while a cutoff low \nremains entrenched over far W TX and SE NM. Our forecast area lies \nbetween both of these features, resulting in continued dry NE flow \naloft. The unseasonably strong ridge is expected to remain the \npredominant feature through this weekend as hghts aloft peak around \n590-591 dam which is near the 97th percent of climatology for early \nOctober. Due to expansive positive hght anomalies, temperatures \nare expected to reach up to 3-5 degrees above normal with lower \ndesert highs in the upper 90s to low 100s today and Sunday. The \nhottest temperatures will reside along and west of the Lower \nColorado River valley where highs will peak in the 100-103 degree \nrange. Overnight lows will also remain on the warmer side, mainly \nin the mid 70s to around 80 degrees through at least Monday \nmorning which can be attributed to elevated winds that will occur\nprior to sunrise. These above normal low/high temperatures will \nresult in a Moderate HeatRisk across the western deserts.\n \nDue to an increased sfc pressure gradient, winds will continue to \nremain breezy with gusts reaching 20-30 mph at times across \nsouthcentral and southwest AZ. The strongest gusts look to be from \nearly morning through the mid afternoon time period. It is also \npossible that locally higher gusts up to 35 mph could materialize \nfor a few hours across the higher terrain of E And SE AZ as well as \nin southwestern AZ as winds become channeled through terrain features.\nTherefore, patchy blowing dust will be possible, especially around\nthe Yuma area. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nHeading into next week, deterministic and ensemble guidance show the \nunseasonably strong ridge weaken and retrograde wwd as an upper-lvl\ntrough progresses through the Intermountain West. This will allow\ntemperatures to dip a few degrees on Monday afternoon, although \nhighs will still remain slightly above normal. The aforementioned \ntrough will provide a temporary weakness in 500 mb hghts over \nsouthcentral AZ early next week, however positive hght anomalies \nwill still exist over SE CA and SW AZ which will maintain highs in\nthe low 100s. As ridging aloft begins to expand back over N AZ by\nmid-week, significant warming is progged to arrive with highs \nacross the western deserts topping out around 100-105 degrees and \napproaching 100 degrees for the central AZ deserts on Wednesday \nand Thursday. These will likely be the hottest days of the \nforecast period before another trough potentially arrives onto the\nwest coast by Friday into next weekend. Overall, subsidence aloft\ncoupled with N-NE flow in the mid-lvls will maintain a dry \nforecast with near zero rain chances each day next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1744Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nGusty easterly winds will prevail through Sunday with mostly \nclear skies. Winds will be strongest in the morning hours through\nearly afternoon, with speeds mostly around 10-15 kts and gusts\naround 20-25 kts. Winds will be slightly stronger Sunday morning,\nwith gusts briefly reaching up as high as 30-35 kts between\n15-18Z. Winds will stay elevated through the overnight hours\naround 7-11 kts with an occasional gust. Minor LLWS impacts may\ndevelop overnight with 1000-2000 ft AGL winds increasing to 30-35\nkts, but shear magnitudes are expected to remain below TAF\nthreshold.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo significant aviation weather impacts expected under clear \nskies. E-NE winds are expected through most of the TAF period at \nboth terminals with speeds mostly at or below 7-12 kts, and \noccasional gusts to 15-20 kts at KBLH this afternoon. Lighter\nspeeds, with VRB conditions, are expected during the overnight\nhours. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions with slightly above normal temperatures are \nanticipated through this weekend before further warming occurs\ninto the middle of next week. MinRHs will mostly range from \n15-25% over the western districts to 25-35% for the eastern \ndistricts with fair to good overnight recovery area-wide. A \npersistent breezy easterly gradient wind will continue through \nat least Monday. Expect winds to remain elevated around 8-12 mph \nwith early morning and afternoon gusts up to 20-30 mph. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Salerno\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...Benedict\nFIRE WEATHER...Salerno\n",
